From e07dc8086f0047a5430d2da5e7a0ca3d8f803e2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damian Dzierzawski <dzierzawskii98.dam@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 19 Jun 2026 08:21:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] virtio-core: add legacy INTx interrupt support
 (aarch64/riscv64)

virtio-core was MSI-X-only: probe asserted the device supported MSI-X and
arch::enable_msix was unimplemented!() on aarch64/riscv64, so every virtio
driver aborted at startup on those platforms. E-OS aarch64 has no usable MSI
controller -- nvmed/xhcid bind via legacy INTx -- which is why virtio-rngd
crashed in arch::enable_msix right after spawning.

Add an INTx fallback, selected per-arch:
  - arch::setup_interrupt() returns (irq_handle, InterruptMethod). x86 keeps
    MSI-X unchanged; aarch64/riscv64 allocate the device's legacy INTx line via
    irq_helpers::pci_allocate_interrupt_vector (the nvmed / R-401c pattern).
  - probe maps the ISR-status PCI capability and passes it + the method to the
    transport.
  - In INTx mode the queue/config vectors are programmed to
    VIRTIO_MSI_NO_VECTOR, and the IRQ thread acknowledges each level-triggered
    interrupt by reading the ISR register (de-asserts the device line) and
    read+write-EOIing the kernel IRQ handle. The MSI-X path is unchanged.

Validated under QEMU with -device virtio-rng-pci:
  - x86_64 (MSI-X): no regression -- virtio-rngd binds, pulls entropy, seeds
    /scheme/rand, boots to login.
  - aarch64 (INTx): virtio-rngd now binds on a shared INTx line, pulls 32 bytes
    of entropy, and seeds /scheme/rand -- where it matters most, since randd
    otherwise seeds from a zero (INSECURE) seed without FEAT_RNG.

This unblocks all virtio drivers (blk/net/gpu/rng) on aarch64.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit e1d5ed8168580d722915421b85d69f0a0641be00)
